+# Contract: End-of-Session Signal Request
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](../spec.md) | **Date**: 2026-10-26 | **Status**: Normative for this feature
+
+This is the wire contract between the host hook (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/session_hook.py`) and
+the GraphHelm Runtime for the `agent_session_ended` signal. The only change this feature makes to
+the contract is the addition of one request header, `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session`.
+
+## Transport
+
+| Property | Value |
+|----------|-------|
+| Method | `POST` |
+| Path | `/v1/executions/{execution}/signal` — `execution` URL-quoted with `safe=''` |
+| Base URL | `GRAPHHELM_RUNTIME_URL` (default `http://127.0.0.1:8791`), trailing slash stripped |
+| Auth | `Authorization: Bearer {token}` where the token is read from `GRAPHHELM_TOKEN_FILE` |
+| Content-Type | `application/json` |
+| Timeout | 1.0 s (client-side, unchanged) |
+| Redirects | Not followed (client suppresses them) |
+
+## Request body
+
+Unchanged JSON object:
+
+```json
+{ "signal": <signal object> }
+```
+
+The `signal` object is produced by `_signal()` and is unchanged by this feature. Its shape:
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "session-end-v2-<24-hex>",
+  "source": { "type": "tool", "id": "<host>-session-<session>" },
+  "type": "agent_session_ended",
+  "severity": "low",
+  "description": "<actor> session ended; task outcome was not verified by this hook.",
+  "evidence": ["<execution>"],
+  "emittedAt": "<ISO-8601 with timezone>"
+}
+```
+
+When a declared node binding is present, a descriptive suffix is appended to `description`; it does
+not affect any header (spec Edge Case "Declared node binding present").
+
+## Request headers
+
+### Unchanged (present before and after, values unchanged — FR-003)
+
+| Header | Value |
+|--------|-------|
+| `Authorization` | `Bearer {token}` |
+| `Content-Type` | `application/json` |
+| `X-GraphHelm-Actor` | composite actor identity `{host}-session-{session}` |
+| `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Type` | `agent` |
+| `Idempotency-Key` | `session-end-v2-<24-hex>` derived from `(execution, session, host, origin)` |
+
+### Added by this feature (FR-001)
+
+| Header | Value | Rules |
+|--------|-------|-------|
+| `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` | The validated host session identifier from the hook payload `session_id` | Equals the value already used to build `X-GraphHelm-Actor`'s session portion and to scope local state (FR-002). Raw session id only: no host prefix, no execution id, no secret/token material (FR-006). Non-empty. Byte-for-byte identical across retried deliveries (SC-004). Present on the end request **only**; absent from the start request (FR-004). |
+
+**Case-sensitivity note**: HTTP header field names are case-insensitive. The normative name is
+`X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session`. Under Python 3.11's stdlib client the emitted bytes are the title-cased
+`X-Graphhelm-Actor-Session` (see research.md R-4); a conforming Runtime must match this header
+case-insensitively, exactly as it already does for the existing actor headers.
+
+## Response contract
+
+Unchanged. The client requires a JSON object with top-level `"ok": true` and `"data"` containing
+`executionId` equal to the bound execution and `signalId` equal to the stored idempotency key. Any
+other response (non-`ok`, wrong fields, oversized, transport error, redirect) is treated as
+unobserved: the receipt stays `delivered=false` and delivery remains retryable with identical bytes.
+
+## Idempotency & retry semantics
+
+- The `(signal body, Idempotency-Key, X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session)` triple is stable across all
+  attempts for one logical session end, because every member derives from the identity tuple
+  `(execution, session, host, origin)` that `_validate_delivery()` re-proves on each retry.
+- A change of Runtime origin changes the identity tuple, which yields a different
+  `Idempotency-Key` (existing behavior, asserted by `test_runtime_origin_is_part_of_delivery_receipt_scope`).
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- The session-start briefing request (`GET /v1/executions/{execution}/briefing`) — unchanged.
+- The Runtime-side parsing/acceptance of the new header (Runtime presence subsystem) — consumer
+  side, not part of this feature's hook-boundary change.
+- Signal body contents, token handling, redirect policy, timeouts, and state schema.
