+# Research: Session End Host-Session Attribution Header
+
+**Feature**: [specs/001-bench](spec.md) | **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+All NEEDS CLARIFICATION items from the Technical Context were resolved by direct inspection of
+the checked-out sources and one on-machine probe. No item remains open. Governance reference:
+`AGENTS.md` at the repository root (substituted for the absent project constitution file).
+
+## R-1: Exact attachment point for the new header
+
+**Question**: Where exactly does the end-of-session request get its attribution headers?
+
+**Decision**: The headers are an inline dict literal passed to `request(...)` inside
+`_end_impl()` in `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/session_hook.py` (line 349):
+
+```python
+reply = request(f"{url}/v1/executions/{...}/signal", token_from_file(token_file), "POST",
+                {"signal": signal},
+                {"X-GraphHelm-Actor": signal["source"]["id"],
+                 "X-GraphHelm-Actor-Type": "agent",
+                 "Idempotency-Key": signal_id})
+```
+
+The new entry `"X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session": session` is added to this same dict. There is no
+other code path that posts the end signal; the start flow (`_start_impl`) issues a briefing GET
+without any of these headers.
+
+**Rationale**: One insertion point covers first attempts, retries, and mismatched-ack redeliveries
+(boundaries B1-B3), because all of them go through `_end_impl`. Adding it inline satisfies FR-005
+(no new helper) and FR-003 (additive only).
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+
+- A shared "attribution headers" builder used by both start and end — rejected: FR-005 forbids a
+  new reusable header-helper abstraction, and FR-004 keeps the start flow header-free.
+- Extending `request()` itself to derive the header from state — rejected: larger surface, would
+  couple transport to identity, and still needs an explicit opt-out for the start flow.
+
+## R-2: Value source for `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session`
+
+**Question**: Which variable carries "the validated host session identifier supplied by the host
+hook payload" (FR-002)?
+
+**Decision**: The local `session` in `_end_impl()`, i.e. the return of `_session(payload)`.
+`_session()` enforces the strict identifier grammar
+`[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._:-]{0,127}\Z` and, when `GRAPHHELM_SESSION_ID` is configured, an equality
+check against it — otherwise it raises before any HTTP traffic. This is the same value already
+used to build the composite actor (`f"{host}-session-{session}"` in `_signal()`), to key the local
+state file (`state_path(execution, session, host, origin)`), and to scope delivery identity.
+
+**Rationale**: Using the same validated string guarantees FR-006 (raw session id only — no host
+prefix, execution id, or secrets) by construction: the value cannot differ from what is already
+proven non-secret on the wire via the actor header and idempotency key derivation.
+
+**Alternatives considered**:
+
+- Reading `delivery["sessionId"]` from persisted state — semantically identical
+  (`_validate_delivery()` already proves `delivery.sessionId == session`) but indirection with no
+  benefit; rejected as unnecessary surface.
+- Parsing the session out of the composite actor string — rejected: reintroduces the exact
+  reverse-engineering the spec says the Runtime should stop doing.
+
+## R-3: Retry stability (SC-004)
+
+**Question**: Does the new header stay byte-for-byte identical across retried deliveries?
+
+**Decision**: Yes, by construction, with no extra mechanism. On a retry, `_read_state()` reloads
+the delivery receipt and `_validate_delivery()` raises unless the stored `sessionId` equals the
+freshly validated `session` from the current payload (identity mismatch → no POST sent). The header
+is derived from that same validated `session`, and the stored signal body / `Idempotency-Key` are
+already asserted stable by the existing tests
+(`test_failed_delivery_is_retryable_with_the_same_signal_bytes`,
+`test_killed_end_hook_releases_lock_and_retries_same_signal`). The test extension should therefore
+assert header equality across the two attempted POSTs, mirroring the existing idempotency-key
+assertion.
+
+**Rationale**: The stability question reduces to "is the value source stable?", and R-2 pins the
+source to a value the receipt-validation machinery already treats as stable identity.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: Persisting the header value in the delivery receipt — rejected: the
+value is derivable and already implicitly stored (`sessionId`); storing it twice would add schema
+surface the spec excludes.
+
+## R-4: On-wire header casing observed by the stub
+
+**Question**: With what exact key spelling does the new header arrive in the in-process stub's
+`dict(self.headers)`, so the test asserts the right thing?
+
+**Decision**: `X-Graphhelm-Actor-Session`. Measured on this machine (Python 3.11.9): when
+`urllib.request` sends a request, `AbstractHTTPHandler.do_request_` rewrites header names with
+`str.title()` (`headers = {name.title(): val for name, val in headers.items()}`), so a header set
+as `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` leaves the client as `X-Graphhelm-Actor-Session` and the
+`http.server` stub records exactly that key (raw wire bytes verified by probe; the existing suite
+already relies on this — e.g. line 186 asserts `sent[0][2]["X-Graphhelm-Actor-Type"]` for a header
+sent as `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Type`).
+
+**Rationale**: A probe POST through the same stack (`urllib.request.Request` → `ThreadingHTTPServer`
+handler) returned recorded keys `X-Graphhelm-Actor`, `X-Graphhelm-Actor-Type`, `Idempotency-Key`.
+The spec's wire name `X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` remains the normative contract name
+(case-insensitive per HTTP); only the stub-side assertion uses the title-cased key.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: Asserting via a case-insensitive dict lookup in the stub — more
+robust to transport changes but alters the shared fixture's recording behavior for all tests; the
+spec's smallest-change boundary prefers the established exact-key pattern already used at line 186.
+
+## R-5: Start-flow isolation (FR-004, SC-003)
+
+**Question**: Can the change accidentally touch the start request's header set?
+
+**Decision**: No. The only edit is inside `_end_impl()`'s POST call. The start flow's sole HTTP
+call is the briefing GET in `_start_impl()` (line 268), which passes no extra headers dict and is
+not modified; its start request therefore keeps exactly `Authorization` + `Content-*` defaults.
+No previously passing test changes result because no assertion anywhere references
+`X-GraphHelm-Actor-Session` yet, and the end-request assertions being added are pure additions to
+an existing test.
+
+**Rationale**: Verified by reading both flows; the test suite's start tests
+(e.g. `test_bound_start_reads_live_briefing_but_does_not_promote_objective_to_instructions`)
+assert specific header values and remain satisfied unchanged.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: A negative test asserting the start request lacks the header — a
+reasonable add, but the spec bounds test changes to extending the *end* test (FR-007); the full
+suite staying green plus R-5's flow isolation is the required observer, and optional hardening
+belongs in tasks, not the plan's mandatory surface.
+
+## R-6: Success-criteria observability mapping
+
+**Decision**: Every success criterion has an observer available from this checkout:
+
+| Criterion | Observer |
+|-----------|----------|
+| SC-001 | Extended end-request test: header present, non-empty, value == payload `session_id` (`session-123`) in the recorded POST. |
+| SC-002 | Same extended test passing + full hook suite (`python plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_session_hook.py`) green; baseline green run measured on this checkout (see quickstart.md). |
+| SC-003 | Start-flow code path unmodified (R-5) + start tests green in the full-suite run. |
+| SC-004 | Retry-stability assertion on the header added to the existing retry test (`test_failed_delivery_is_retryable_with_the_same_signal_bytes`), mirroring its idempotency-key assertion. |
+
+**Rationale**: No criterion depends on the real Runtime or network; the in-process stub records the
+exact header dict, so presence/value are directly observable, not inferred.
+
+**Alternatives considered**: A live Runtime check against `adapters/tool-host` — out of scope
+(spec bounds the work to hook + its tests) and would require building the Rust workspace,
+explicitly disfavored for verification here.
